l, and daily study time. This is the result your project should produce.\",\n    \"connection\": \"Keep this profile as the first item in your learning journal. Tomorrow you will learn why text and numbers behave differently.\",\n    \"codeTask\": \"Change the name to your own name and minutes_per_day to 30. Run the code. Check that the name and study time change, while the goal stays the same.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 2,\n    \"title\": \"Text and numbers have different jobs\",\n    \"situation\": \"A shop needs a converter. A distance written in a form arrives as text, but multiplication needs a number.\",\n    \"explanation\": \"A type tells the computer what kind of value it has. The number 2 can take part in arithmetic. The text \\\"2\\\" is a character inside quotation marks. Adding two text values joins them: \\\"2\\\" + \\\"2\\\" becomes \\\"22\\\". Convert the text to a number before calculating. Always keep the unit beside the result so another person can understand it.\",\n    \"terms\": [\n      [\"String\", \"A text value, written inside quotation marks.\"],\n      [\"Integer\", \"A whole number, such as 0, 2, or -3.\"],\n      [\"Conversion\", \"Changing a value into another type or unit.\"]\n    ],\n    \"walkthrough\": [\n      \"raw_distance contains the text 2.5. float changes suitable text into a number that can have a fractional part.\",\n      \"The condition rejects negative distances. The calculation multiplies kilometres by 1000.\",\n      \"The print shows both units. The except block displays a useful message if conversion or validation fails.\"\n    ],\n    \"question\": \"A form gives you \\\"2\\\" kilometres as text. Which calculation gives 2000 metres?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      [\n        \"int(\\\"2\\\") * 1000\",\n        \"Yes. int makes the text a number; multiplying by 1000 converts kilometres to metres.\"\n      ],\n      [\"\\\"2\\\" + \\\"1000\\\"\", \"Adding strings joins them into 21000. It does not convert units.\"],\n 