\n    \"question\": \"A ticket is critical and 30 hours old. With the critical rule checked first, what priority should it receive?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      [\n        \"Standard, because every ticket starts there\",\n        \"The program evaluates the conditions. A starting value does not override a true critical rule.\"\n      ],\n      [\n        \"Review and urgent at the same time\",\n        \"An if/elif/else chain chooses one branch. The project asks for exactly one priority.\"\n      ],\n      [\n        \"Urgent\",\n        \"Yes. The first condition matches critical severity, so the later age branch is skipped.\"\n      ]\n    ],\n    \"answer\": 2,\n    \"hints\": [\n      \"Follow the conditions from top to bottom.\",\n      \"After a true if branch, Python skips the related elif and else branches.\"\n    ],\n    \"firstStep\": \"Write three example tickets and their expected priorities: critical, ordinary at exactly 24 hours, and ordinary at 23 hours. Put the critical rule first.\",\n    \"connection\": \"Your converter had input rules. Now you can express rules directly and apply them repeatedly to a queue.\",\n    \"codeTask\": \"Find the age threshold in the example. Test an ordinary ticket at 23, 24, and 25 hours. Keep the rule unchanged and explain why exactly 24 belongs to review.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 4,\n    \"title\": \"Give a small job its own function\",\n    \"situation\": \"A business needs to calculate several quotes without copying the same arithmetic everywhere.\",\n    \"explanation\": \"A function is a named set of instructions for one job. You give it inputs and it can return a result. Think of a calculator button: give it price and quantity, then receive a line total. Returning a value lets other code use it. Printing only displays it. Keep the calculation separate from the way the quote looks on screen.\",\n    \"terms\": [\n      [\"Function\", \"A named, reusable piece of work.\"],\n      [\"Argument\", \"A value you pass into a function.\"],\n      [\"Return\", \"Send a result back to the code that called the function.\"]\n    ],\n    \"walkthrough\": [\n      \"def introduces the calculation and names its inputs.\",\n      \"The calculation uses integer cents. For example, 250 cents means 2.50 in the example currency.\",\n      \"return gives the calculated amount back. A separate print statement displays the result.\"\n    ],\n    \"question\": \"One item costs 250 cents. You buy 3. What should a line-total function return?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      [\n        \"253 cents\",\n        \"Adding price and quantity mixes different meanings. Multiply the price by the number of items.\"\n      ],\n      [\n        \"750 cents\",\n        \"Yes. 250 × 3 = 750 cents. Returning this number lets another function add delivery.\"\n      ],\n      [\n        \"Nothing, because print already returns the total\",\n        \"print displays a value and returns None. The calculation needs its own return statement.\"\n      ]\n    ],\n    \"answer\": 1,\n    \"hints\": [\n      \"A line total is price multiplied by quantity.\",\n      \"Keep the answer in cents: 250 + 250 + 250.\"\n    ],\n    \"firstStep\": \"Write one example quote by hand with two items and a delivery fee, all in cents. Work out the expected grand total before writing functions.\",\n    \"connection\": \"Use conditions from Day 3 to reject invalid quantities. This calculator can later support a web form without changing its arithmetic.\",\n    \"codeTask\": \"Keep the existing example and assertion. Add print(line_total_cents(250, 0)) below it. Check that this extra line prints 0. Explain why returning the total is useful.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 5,\n    \"title\": \"Choose how to organise your data\",\n    \"situation\": \"Two customers have the same name. Your contact directory must keep both records.\",\n    \"explanation\": \"A list stores a sequence of values. A dictionary connects keys to values, like looking up a