n the example and check that the original record still exists.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 6,\n    \"title\": \"Save data so it survives a restart\",\n    \"situation\": \"Your contact directory works, but closing the program loses its records.\",\n    \"explanation\": \"Values in a running program usually disappear when the program stops. A file can keep them for a later run. JSON is a text format for structured data. Saving turns records into file contents; loading reads those contents back. A missing file and a damaged file are different problems. Handle both deliberately so a recovery step does not silently destroy existing work.\",\n    \"terms\": [\n      [\"Persistence\", \"Keeping data after the program stops.\"],\n      [\"JSON\", \"A text format used to exchange and store structured values.\"],\n      [\"Exception\", \"A reported problem that interrupts the normal flow of instructions.\"]\n    ],\n    \"walkthrough\": [\n      \"The example turns contact data into JSON text and reads it back.\",\n      \"Parsing checks whether the text is valid JSON. It does not guarantee the fields your app needs are present.\",\n      \"isinstance checks that the top-level value is a dictionary. Field-level validation and local file save/load are further project work.\"\n    ],\n    \"question\": \"Your contacts print correctly. You close the app, reopen it, and they are gone. What was missing?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      [\n        \"More print statements\",\n        \"Printing shows a result but does not save an application record for a later run.\"\n      ],\n      [\n        \"A different contact name\",\n        \"The data is lost because it was only held during that run. The name does not change this.\"\n      ],\n      [\n        \"Saving records and loading them on the next run\",\n        \"Yes. Persistence needs a place to store data and a way to read it back.\"\n      ]\n    ],\n    \"answer\": 2,\n    \"hints\": [\n      \"Separate what exists in memory from