et a user record or inspect an expense.\"\n      ],\n      [\n        \"Add one expense, save it, and show it after restarting\",\n        \"Yes. This proves one complete useful path, including persistence.\"\n      ],\n      [\n        \"Add an AI assistant before storing expenses\",\n        \"First make the basic expense workflow reliable. AI does not replace the missing record flow.\"\n      ]\n    ],\n    \"answer\": 1,\n    \"hints\": [\n      \"Choose something a user can actually finish.\",\n      \"A first complete path can be add → save → restart → list.\"\n    ],\n    \"firstStep\": \"Write the exact terminal conversation for adding one fictional expense and listing it again after a restart. Use that conversation as your first test.\",\n    \"connection\": \"Combine Days 2–6: types, rules, functions, collections, and files. This becomes the expense app you will test and give a web interface later.\",\n    \"codeTask\": \"Add a fictional expense of 150 cents to the example. Predict the new total first, then run the calculation and compare.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 8,\n    \"title\": \"Keep a history you can review\",\n    \"situation\": \"You need to change your expense tracker without losing a working version or confusing a teammate.\",\n    \"explanation\": \"Git records selected versions of your files. A branch lets you work on a change separately. A diff shows the lines that changed, so you can review them before committing. A commit records a local snapshot; pushing sends commits to a remote repository. These are different actions. For this lesson, use a practice repository and keep the change small.\",\n    \"terms\": [\n      [\"Diff\", \"A comparison showing added and removed lines.\"],\n      [\"Commit\", \"A saved snapshot in Git history with a message.\"],\n      [\"Pull request\", \"A proposal to review and merge changes from a branch.\"]\n    ],\n    \"walkthrough\": [\n      \"The commands create a feature branch and inspect the current state.\",\n      \"git diff shows changes before staging. git diff --staged shows what the next commit will contain.\",\n      \"git commit records the staged changes locally. Uploading or merging is a separate step.\"\n    ],\n    \"question\": \"You changed a file but have not run git add. Where can you review that change?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      [\n        \"git diff\",\n        \"Yes. git diff shows unstaged changes to tracked files. New untracked files also need inspecting directly.\"\n      ],\n      [\n        \"Only on GitHub after merging\",\n        \"You can inspect changes locally before uploading or merging them.\"\n      ],\n      [\n        \"git commit automatically explains every change\",\n        \"A commit records staged work; it does not replace reviewing the actual changed lines.\"\n      ]\n    ],\n    \"answer\": 0,\n    \"hints\": [\n      \"Review the file before you put it in the next snapshot.\",\n      \"git diff checks unstaged changes; git diff --staged checks staged changes.\"\n    ],\n    \"firstStep\": \"Use a copy of your expense project. Run git status, then write down the one validation behaviour you want to change before creating a branch.\",\n    \"connection\": \"Version your Day 7 tracker. Your project deliverable today is a small reviewed change and its history, so use your terminal rather than the browser code runner.\",\n    \"codeTask\": \"In your practice repository, change one validation message. Inspect the unstaged diff, stage that file, then inspect the staged diff.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 9,\n    \"title\": \"Follow a request and its response\",\n    \"situation\": \"Your weather tool asks another service for information. Sometimes the reply is missing a temperature.\",\n    \"explanation\": \"An API is an agreed way for programs to communicate. With HTTP, your program sends a request and the server returns a response. The response has a status and may contain JSON data. A successful network response can still contain unexpected fields. Check both the response status and the data you need before displaying a result.\",\n    \"terms\": [\n      [\"Request\", \"A message asking a server to do or return something.\"],\n      [\"Response\", \"The server's reply to that message.\"],\n      [\"Fixture\", \"A saved example used to test behaviour without a live service.\"]\n    ],\n    \"walkthrough\": [\n      \"The example uses a saved JSON response, so it does not call a live weather service.\",\n      \"It reads the data and checks the fields needed for a weather result.\",\n      \"The adapter converts outside data into a shape your own program understands.\"\n    ],\n    \"question\": \"A response says status 200 but its JSON has no temperature. What should the weather app do?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      [\n        \"Show 0 degrees without explanation\",\n        \"A missing measurement does not mean zero. That would invent information.\"\n      ],\n      [\n        \"Assume success because 200 was returned\",\n        \"The network request succeeded, but your application still needs to validate the fields.\"\n      ],\n      [\n        \"Show a clear missing-data result\",\n        \"Yes. Check the content as well as the HTTP status and handle missing measurements explicitly.\"\n      ]\n    ],\n    \"answer\": 2,\n    \"hints\": [\n      \"Separate delivery of a response from usefulness of its contents.\",\n      \"A missing temperature is unknown, not zero.\"\n    ],\n    \"firstStep\": \"Draw client → request → service → response → display. Write the expected result for both a complete reply and a reply missing temperature.\",\n    \"connection\": \"Reuse your validation and error-handling skills. Offline fixtures let you test failures repeatedly before connecting a real service.\",\n    \"codeTask\": \"Remove temperature from the saved response in the example. Run it and check whether it reports missing data clearly rather than inventing a value.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 10,\n    \"title\": \"Make setup repeatable\",\n    \"situation\": \"Your app works on your laptop. A teammate cannot run it because their Python packages are different.\",\n    \"explanation\": \"A project depends on more than its code: the language version, packages, settings, and run commands matter too. A virtual environment keeps one project's Python packages separate from other projects. A dependency file records what needs installing. Clear setup instructions help someone recreate the environment instead of guessing what your laptop contains.\",\n    \"terms\": [\n      [\"Dependency\", \"A package or tool your project needs.\"],\n      [\"Virtual environment\", \"An isolated Python environment for a project.\"],\n      [\"Reproducible\", \"Possible to repeat using the documented inputs and setup.\"]\n    ],\n    \"walkthrough\": [\n      \"The diagnostic prints the Python major version used for this run.\",\n      \"It reports whether the interpreter is using a virtual environment.\",\n      \"It demonstrates inspecting setup, not creating an environment. Create and test that locally for the project.\"\n    ],\n    \"question\": \"A teammate has your source files but no installed packages. What helps them run the app reliably?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      [\n        \"A screenshot of your desktop\",\n        \"A screenshot does not give them package versions or working run commands.\"\n      ],\n      [\n        \"Documented language version, dependencies, setup, and run commands\",\n        \"Yes. They can recreate and verify the environment from those instructions.\"\n      ],\n      [\n        \"Your private API key committed in Git\",\n        \"Credentials are personal settings, not reusable source code. Use placeholders and local settings.\"\n      ]\n    ],\n    \"answer\": 1,\n    \"hints\": [\n      \"Think about every input a fresh machine needs.\",\n      \"Code + runtime + dependencies + settings + commands make a useful setup guide.\"\n    ],\n    \"firstStep\": \"Open your tracker README as if you had never seen the project. List every missing setup step before trying a fresh local environment.\",\n    \"connection\": \"Your Git repository keeps source history. Now make that source runnable by another person. Browser runtime diagnostics describe the browser environment, not your laptop.\",\n    \"codeTask\": \"Run the diagnostic and read its two fields. Then run the downloaded diagnostic locally and explain why the environment result may differ.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 11,\n    \"title\": \"Keep related state and rules together\",\n    \"situation\": \"A support ticket should move between allowed states. A random string should not become its status.\",\n    \"explanation\": \"An object groups some data with actions that use that data. A class describes how to make those objects. For a ticket, the data might be its ID and status; an action might close it. The useful part is keeping state changes behind clear rules. Use a class when this helps understanding; small functions can still be the right choice elsewhere.\",\n    \"terms\": [\n      [\"Class\", \"A definition used to create objects with related data and actions.\"],\n      [\"State\", \"The values describing something at a particular moment.\"],\n      [\"Method\", \"A function attached to an object.\"]\n    ],\n    \"walkthrough\": [\n      \"The class creates a ticket with an open starting state.\",\n      \"close checks whether the current state is open before changing it to closed.\",\n      \"The prints show the state before and after closing. The project adds a fuller state policy and validation.\"\n    ],\n    \"question\": \"A ticket only allows open and closed. Someone asks to set its status to banana. What should happen?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      [\n        \"Reject the unsupported state and keep the previous valid state\",\n        \"Yes. The transition rule protects the object's data from an invalid state.\"\n      ],\n 